          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a concrete driveway need rebar?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Reinforcement does not stop concrete cracking, but it holds the slab together when cracks form and prevents the halves separating or heaving. Wire mesh or a rebar grid typically adds $0.25 to $0.60 per square foot — a small share of the total and worth including, particularly on expansive clay soils or in freeze-thaw climates.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a concrete driveway last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Thirty years or more is realistic for a properly built slab on a well-compacted base, and many last considerably longer. The variables that shorten that are a poorly prepared subgrade, insufficient thickness for the traffic, missing or badly placed control joints, and de-icing salt used in the first winter before the concrete has fully cured.",
          },
