        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Two quotes for the same driveway can differ by half, and usually not because one contractor is greedy. Concrete pricing is dominated by things you cannot see from the street: how deep the excavation goes, what the base is made of, how thick the slab is, and whether there is steel in it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Concrete Driveway Cost at a Glance
        </h2>

        <p>
          For a plain, broom-finished slab at the standard four-inch thickness, most US markets sit between <strong>$8 and $18 per square foot installed</strong>. That figure includes excavation, a compacted gravel base, forming, the concrete itself, placing and finishing, and control joints. It does not usually include demolition of an existing driveway, which is quoted separately.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Installed Cost, 2026</p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$8-18</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft, plain finish</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$4.8-10.8k</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical 600 sq ft driveway</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2-6</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft to demolish the old one</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">30+ yrs</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Realistic lifespan when built right</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Driveway sizes vary more than people expect, and the square footage is what the whole estimate hangs on. A single-car driveway of roughly 10 by 20 feet is 200 square feet. A standard two-car driveway at 20 by 30 feet is 600. A longer rural drive can easily reach 1,000 to 1,500 square feet, at which point every dollar per square foot is worth a conversation.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
