        <h2 id="by-finish" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Cost by Finish
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Broom Finish — $8 to $18 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          The default and, for a driveway, a genuinely good choice. A broom drawn across the surface while the concrete is still plastic leaves a fine texture that provides traction when wet and hides minor imperfections. It is the cheapest finish because it is the fastest, and it is the one most likely to still look acceptable in fifteen years.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Exposed Aggregate — $10 to $20 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          The surface paste is washed off shortly after placement to reveal the stone underneath. It looks considerably more expensive than it is, wears well, and grips in the wet. The extra cost covers the retarder, the wash-off, and the tighter timing window the crew has to hit. It also hides staining better than any other finish, which matters on a surface that will see oil drips.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Stamped Concrete — $12 to $25 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          Patterned mats pressed into fresh concrete produce a stone, brick or slate look. It is the most expensive common finish and the most skill-dependent: the crew has to place, colour, stamp and release across the whole pour on a fixed clock. It also carries an ongoing commitment, because the colour is protected by a film-forming sealer that needs recoating every two to four years. Our <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/">stamped concrete sealer guide</a> covers that maintenance.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Coloured and Integral-Pigment Concrete — Add $1 to $4 per Square Foot</h3>
        <p>
          Pigment mixed into the truck load colours the concrete throughout rather than just at the surface, so wear does not expose grey underneath. The premium depends heavily on the colour: earth tones are cheap, deep charcoals and reds cost considerably more pigment per yard.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-drives-price" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. What Actually Drives the Price
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Slab Thickness</h3>
        <p>
          Four inches is standard for cars and light trucks. Five or six is appropriate where an RV, a loaded work truck, or delivery vehicles will park. Every extra inch adds roughly 8 to 12 percent to the concrete cost — a modest premium against the cost of a slab cracking under loads it was never designed for. If heavy vehicles are in your future, this is the upgrade worth buying before any decorative finish.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Base Underneath</h3>
        <p>
          Four to six inches of compacted crushed gravel is what actually carries the load; the slab spreads it. Base work is invisible in the finished job and expensive to do properly, which makes it the most commonly skimped line item in a cheap quote. On poor or expansive soils the base may need to be deeper still, and a driveway built on inadequately compacted fill will crack regardless of how good the concrete was.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Reinforcement</h3>
        <p>
          Wire mesh or a rebar grid typically adds $0.25 to $0.60 per square foot. Reinforcement does not prevent cracking — concrete cracks, that is why control joints exist — but it holds the pieces in plane when it does, which is the difference between a hairline you barely notice and two slabs at different heights.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Site Access and Excavation</h3>
        <p>
          A truck that can back up to the forms and chute directly is the cheap case. Anything else — pumping over a house, wheelbarrowing down a slope, working around a tight gate — adds labour or equipment. Slope, drainage work, tree roots and utilities all sit in this category, and it is why two identical-looking driveways in the same town can price differently.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Region and Season</h3>
        <p>
          Labour rates dominate regional variation, and the same slab can differ by 40 percent between a rural southern market and a coastal metro. Season matters too: crews are busiest and priciest in late spring and summer, and a job booked for early autumn often prices better. Cold-weather pours need blankets or admixtures, which adds cost rather than saving it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="demolition" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Removing the Old Driveway
        </h2>

        <p>
          Demolition and haul-away typically runs <strong>$2 to $6 per square foot</strong>, so clearing a 600-square-foot driveway usually adds somewhere between $1,200 and $3,600 before a single yard of new concrete arrives. Thicker slabs cost more to break, heavily reinforced ones cost more again because the steel has to be cut, and disposal fees vary widely by region.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two things reduce it. First, ask whether the contractor can crush and reuse the old slab as base material on site — where it is permitted, it removes both haul-away and imported gravel from the bill. Second, ask whether the existing slab can stay. If it is structurally sound and simply looks tired, an overlay or resurfacing costs a fraction of replacement, and our <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">driveway resurfacing cost guide</a> covers when that is a legitimate option rather than a way to postpone the real work.
        </p>

        <p>
          What rules resurfacing out is movement. Sections at different heights, cracks that open and close seasonally, or a slab that rocks under a vehicle are all subgrade problems, and putting new material over them buys a year or two at most.
        </p>

        <h2 id="vs-asphalt" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Concrete vs Asphalt Over Thirty Years
        </h2>

        <p>
          Concrete typically costs 40 to 60 percent more to install than asphalt, and that difference is what most comparisons stop at. It is the wrong place to stop, because the two surfaces have completely different maintenance profiles.
        </p>

        <p>
          Asphalt wants resealing every two to four years — a real recurring cost, whether you pay a contractor or buy the pails yourself — and generally needs replacing at 15 to 20 years. Concrete wants sealing every few years at most, and many owners never seal a plain broom-finished slab at all beyond keeping de-icing salt off it. Over a thirty-year horizon the totals converge considerably, and in cold, salted climates the maintenance question matters more than the installation quote.
        </p>

        <p>
          Where asphalt keeps a genuine edge is in very cold regions, where its flexibility handles frost heave better than a rigid slab, and in upfront affordability when the budget simply does not stretch. Our <a href="/asphalt-vs-concrete-driveway/">asphalt vs concrete comparison</a> works through the whole decision, and the <a href="/asphalt-driveway-cost/">asphalt driveway cost guide</a> gives the equivalent numbers for that side.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Asphalt</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Install per sq ft</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8-18</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4-10</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lifespan</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30+ years</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-20 years</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Maintenance cycle</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seal occasionally</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reseal every 2-4 years</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Usable after pour</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 days for vehicles</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 days</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold-climate behaviour</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rigid, salt-sensitive</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible, handles heave</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What About Pavers?</h3>
        <p>
          Interlocking pavers sit above both surfaces on price, commonly $12 to $30 per square foot installed, and they solve a problem neither poured surface does: individual units can be lifted and reset, so a settled area is a repair rather than a replacement. They also never crack, because the joints absorb movement that would split a slab. The offsetting cost is maintenance — joint sand needs topping up and the field wants resealing periodically — and a badly built base shows up faster in pavers than in concrete, because every unit can move independently. If the appeal is the look rather than the repairability, a stamped or exposed-aggregate slab usually gets closer for less money.
        </p>

        <h2 id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Reading a Quote Properly
        </h2>

        <p>
          A quote that says "concrete driveway, $6,400" tells you almost nothing. Six specifics turn it into something you can compare against another bid.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Slab thickness in inches.</strong> Four is standard; if a bid is unusually cheap, this is the first place to look.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Base depth and material.</strong> Ask for inches of compacted crushed stone, not "we prep the ground". A bid with no base line item is not the same product as one with six inches of compacted aggregate.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Reinforcement.</strong> Mesh, rebar grid, or none — and if none, why not for your soil type.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Concrete strength.</strong> Usually expressed in psi. Residential driveways commonly specify 3,500 to 4,000 psi, and in freeze-thaw regions an air-entrained mix is what protects the surface from spalling.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Control joints.</strong> Spacing and how they are formed. Joints let the slab crack where you decided rather than where it chose. Spacing beyond about 10 to 12 feet on a four-inch slab invites random cracking.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>What is excluded.</strong> Demolition, permits, drainage work and grading are frequently separate. The gap between two bids often turns out to be one of these, not the concrete.
        </p>

        <p>
          Get three bids, and treat the outlier in either direction as the one to ask questions about rather than the one to discount automatically. A quote well above the others sometimes reflects a contractor who has spotted a drainage or subgrade problem the others intend to pour straight over. A quote well below usually reflects something missing from the scope, and the honest way to find out is to ask what thickness, base depth and mix strength it assumes.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Ask What Happens in the First Winter</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
            New concrete should not see de-icing salt in its first winter — the surface is still gaining strength and salt drives the freeze-thaw damage that causes spalling. A contractor who volunteers this without prompting is usually a contractor who cares what the slab looks like in year five. Our guide to <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt and concrete</a> covers what to use instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying a Concrete Driveway</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Bids on Price Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheaper bid is often a thinner slab on a shallower base with no steel in it. Get thickness, base depth, reinforcement and psi in writing on every quote, and the spread usually explains itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a Decorative Finish Over a Weak Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stamped concrete on four inches over a poor base is an expensive way to get a patterned crack. Spend on thickness and base first; the finish is what you upgrade with money left over.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Salt in the First Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                De-icing salt on green concrete is the fastest route to a spalled surface, and it is not a warranty claim. Use sand for traction through the first season and keep salt off it entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on It Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foot traffic after a day or two is fine; vehicles need about a week, and heavy trucks longer. Early loading leaves cracks that appear weeks later and get blamed on the mix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Where the Water Goes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A driveway that drains towards the garage or the foundation creates a far more expensive problem than it solved. Slope and drainage should be discussed before the forms go in, not after the first storm.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a concrete driveway cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A plain broom-finished concrete driveway runs roughly $8 to $18 per square foot installed in most US markets, putting a typical 600-square-foot two-car driveway between $4,800 and $10,800. Exposed aggregate typically runs $10 to $20 per square foot and stamped concrete $12 to $25.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is concrete per square foot for a driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Between $8 and $18 installed at a standard four-inch thickness. The spread comes from regional labour rates, excavation and base work, whether reinforcement is included, and truck access. Quotes below about $6 per square foot usually signal a thin slab, no steel, or minimal base preparation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is concrete cheaper than asphalt?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No — concrete typically costs 40 to 60 percent more to install. The trade-off is lifespan and maintenance: 30-plus years with occasional sealing against asphalt's 15 to 20 years plus resealing every two to four. Over the life of the driveway the totals converge.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to remove an old concrete driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Typically $2 to $6 per square foot for demolition and haul-away, so about $1,200 to $3,600 on a 600-square-foot driveway. Thicker or heavily reinforced slabs cost more to break out, and disposal fees vary by region. Ask whether the old slab can be crushed and reused as base on site.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should a concrete driveway be?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Four inches for cars and light trucks; five to six where an RV, work truck or delivery vehicles will use it. Each additional inch adds roughly 8 to 12 percent to the concrete cost, which is one of the better-value upgrades available if heavy vehicles are involved.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a concrete driveway need rebar?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Reinforcement does not stop cracking, but it keeps the slab in plane when cracks form. Mesh or a rebar grid adds roughly $0.25 to $0.60 per square foot — a small share of the total, and worth including on expansive clay soils or in freeze-thaw climates.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a concrete driveway last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Thirty years or more on a well-compacted base, often considerably longer. What shortens it: a poor subgrade, insufficient thickness for the traffic, missing or badly spaced control joints, and de-icing salt in the first winter before the slab has fully cured.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pour a concrete driveway yourself?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Rarely worth it. A full driveway has to be placed, screeded, floated and jointed before it sets — a crew-sized job on a clock you do not control. The saving is typically only 30 to 40 percent of the total, and a failed pour costs more than the labour would have.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

