        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Budget <strong class="text-white">$8 to $18 per square foot</strong> for a plain concrete driveway, plus <strong class="text-white">$2 to $6</strong> per square foot if the old one has to come out. For a standard two-car driveway that is realistically $5,000 to $11,000 before demolition, and more if you want a decorative finish.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Spend the money in the right order: base depth, then slab thickness, then reinforcement, then the finish. A four-inch broom-finished slab on six inches of properly compacted stone will outlive a stamped one poured on bare graded soil, and it will cost less. And keep the salt off it for the first winter — that single habit does more for the twenty-year outcome than any upgrade on the quote.
          </p>
        </div>

